Fake system headers, so that the sources only a Mac can build can at least be
syntax checked anywhere.

Everything macOS only needs Cocoa or IOKit, which no build machine here has.
Until this existed the only check those files had was being read, and reading
does not find what -Werror finds: an unused parameter left behind by a change
is a hard failure of the macOS job, and therefore of the release that job
builds, and it reached that job once.

These headers declare only the types, methods and constants those files
actually name, and they do nothing at all. What "make check-mac" proves is that
the files parse and are warning clean under the project's own flags. It proves
nothing whatever about behaviour.

Adding to them is meant to be done from the compiler's own errors, one at a
time. The one rule that matters: when clang says a name is not declared, check
the real API before declaring it here. A stub written to match the code rather
than the framework turns this check into a mirror, which is worse than having
no check, because it passes.

Covered: src/mac/t150menu.m, src/t150d/hid_darwin.c, src/mac/bootswitch.c,
both command line tools and all four probes. That is every macOS only source.

The USB half of that was once left out on purpose, with this file saying so:
the plug-in and interface interfaces and IOUSBDevRequestTO "would have to be
guessed at, and a wrong guess gives a false answer". They are here now because
the alternative was worse - a change to bootswitch.c, t150ctl, t150boot or any
probe reached a compiler for the first time in CI, on a job that runs late
because it waits for another one's artifact - but the warning still stands and
applies to them most of all. IOUSBDevRequestTO and IOUSBFindInterfaceRequest
are written field for field from the IOKit headers, and the interface methods
from their documented signatures, with the leading void *self those interfaces
really take. If any of that drifts, this check goes quiet rather than wrong,
which is the failure to watch for.

So the macOS job remains the authority. check-mac is the fast one that catches
a warning before it costs a release, not a substitute for building the thing.
